  "mathematical_change": "None; seven local spacing adjustments only.",
  "analytic_review_groups": [
    "Dimension one is a zero-integral Poisson equation; in every dimension extrema bound the scalar without assuming invariant mass.",
    "Comparing extrema of two potentials fixes the scalar, and the positive segment gives a uniformly elliptic operator without a zeroth-order term for uniqueness.",
    "The pointwise torsion estimate is diagonal Young inequality with derivatives outside each wedge subset, uniformly for every positive eta.",
    "Weighted Stokes signs are positive for pJ; the exact derivative of the mixed sum has coefficient n, and the finite recurrence absorbs all remaining weighted masses.",
    "Moser iteration yields a uniformly positive-volume set near the minimum; normalized L1 compactness bounds that minimum rather than assuming exponential integrability.",
    "The mean-zero normalization is differentiable; the adjoint probability determines the scalar in the linear inverse, and Schauder estimates give the implicit-function map.",
    "The Chern torsion switch and completed tensor square give the trace inequality with a derivative-free curvature remainder.",
    "The maximum of log trace minus A*u plus an inverse shift absorbs torsion using the positive gradient square; determinant bounds give both metric inequalities.",
    "A bound for the fixed Laplacian gives W2p and C1alpha estimates; small coordinate segments absorb a trace bound linear in the gradient.",
    "Projection of real Hessians to Hermitian matrices preserves positivity and trace; the concave extension permits local Evans–Krylov and Schauder bootstrapping.",
    "The continuity path has scalar and density bounds independent of the parameter, with uniform positivity preserved in a smooth limit.",
    "The proper exponential equation has a negative zeroth-order term, extrema bounds and an index-zero inverse; its semilinear trace estimate needs no prior derivative bound for u.",
    "First and second derivatives of the scalar use the adjoint at a fixed parameter, without differentiating its measure; the Hermitian square gives strict concavity modulo constants.",
    "Strictly positive adjoint densities give strict Lipschitz extremum inequalities for nonconstant density differences; the continuous extension concerns the scalar only.",
    "Hopf normalization is exactly one plus fibre energy; equality is equivalent to a smooth base density, with the base closed-volume equation fixing its scalar.",
    "The Hessian of the Hopf defect at the background is twice fibre energy; its vanishing is exactly base pullback, without a claim of global convexity.",
    "The Gauduchon ansatz is Aeppli exact and preserves the Gauduchon property when the initial metric is Gauduchon; uniqueness is restricted to the scalar ansatz.",
    "The fixed elliptic drift operator has a positive adjoint and a nonnegative normalized Green kernel, giving the C0 argument its L1 bound.",
    "The compact contact set covers a gradient ball; the determinant equation and lower positive cofactor bound give an upper real-Hessian determinant and hence positive contact-set volume.",
    "The root linearization has factor n/(n-1); its adjoint form includes first derivatives of the auxiliary metric, with all integration-by-parts signs and cancellations retained.",
    "The cofactor matrix has determinant equal to volume density to power n-1; the tensor g need not be positive, while P(g) must be.",
    "Antisymmetric torsion contraction gives the six terms and missing gradient indices, also for n=2; covariant differentiation preserves the needed exclusions.",
    "Ordered eigenvalues give the exact weights and coercivity from the positive background, with determinant bounds forcing a large sum of inverse weights.",
    "The simple-largest-eigenvalue perturbation and concavity squares control third derivatives; the missing torsion indices distinguish the two eigenvalue configurations and fix the order of constant choices.",
    "Cone maximality is stable under local uniform convergence and permits smooth comparison on arbitrary bounded domains; dimension reduction handles the base case.",
    "The Liouville proof separates vanishing directional energy from its uniform lower bound; the Lipschitz annulus estimate converts unweighted energy into a positive weighted matrix.",
    "Gradient rescaling retains the torsion error at inverse scale and the background at inverse-square scale; fixed-Laplacian compactness yields a C1 limit, and touching tests force maximality.",
    "The all-real-Hessian concave extension is uniformly elliptic with Holder x-dependence; the continuity method uses the adjoint inverse and retains positivity in the limit.",
    "Gauduchon scalar variations carry factor 1/(n-1), strict concavity modulo constants and a scalar-only continuous extension; on a Gauduchon surface the scalar is the log-integral formula and the Hessian is minus variance."
  ],
